# Assets live in R2 — git holds text and the manifest

This repo was **10 GB** because renders, cover art and audio were committed for years.
The bytes now live in Cloudflare R2 (`arjuna-badger-prod`); git holds only source text
and `assets.manifest.json`, the contract that maps every asset to its object key.

## The rule

**Never `git add` a heavy binary.** Images, PDFs, EPUBs, audio, video, fonts and archives
belong in R2. A pre-commit gate (`scripts/asset_gate.py`) blocks them, so a violation
fails loudly rather than quietly re-growing the repo. Do not work around the gate —
if it fires, the fix is to put the file in R2, not to bypass it.

**Never commit build output.** `books/*/build/**` is regenerated by `books/*/build.py`
and `tools/render_book.sh`. It is archived offline, never versioned, at any size.

## Adding an asset properly

```bash
./tools/asset_manifest.py build press     # hash it, assign its key
./tools/r2_push.py push press             # upload (immutable, never deletes)
./tools/r2_push.py verify press           # confirm bytes landed
git add assets.manifest.json              # commit the contract, not the bytes
```

## The key scheme (do not improvise)

| class | key | why |
|---|---|---|
| served | `site/<path under public/>` | URLs stay readable and presignable |
| input | `blobs/sha256/<hash>` | immutable, dedupes, survives renames |
| large audio | `audiobooks/<book-id>/<file>` | **live production mapping** |

The `audiobooks/` mapping is served by `saas/api.py` in the platform repo via presigned
redirect. It is in production. Changing it breaks working downloads.

## Balthazar

`balthazar` must never appear in the library — not in the catalogue, not in a credits
table, not as an R2 object under a served prefix, not in any data file under `docs/`.
The bucket is private and the only public door is a narrow Function. Before shipping
anything that renders a data file: `grep -ril balthazar site/public/`.

## Bypass

`ABP_ALLOW_BINARY=1 git commit ...` exists for the rare file that genuinely belongs in
git (a small icon, a test fixture). Reach for it deliberately, never reflexively.
